Context (pp. 529-538 of the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'): Grothendieck is building linear group schemes M_{ρ,σ} attached to a pair of 'elliptic' data ρ, σ. These are presumably the elements of order 4 and 6 (orders 2 and 3 in PSL_2) that generate SL_2(Z) = Z/4 *_{Z/2} Z/6, which is relevant to the loop equation for M_{1,1}. That identification is an inference; the page does not say it. The ingredients are:
- G, a linear group (GL-type, with determinant δ_G);
- T_ρ, a torus (centralizer-type) attached to ρ, with character δ_ρ;
- N'_σ, a normalizer-type group of a torus attached to σ, with character δ_σ and a sign character ε_σ: N'_σ → μ_2 (the component group);
- SG = G^+_{ρ,σ}, the special part;
- R_{ρ,σ}, an auxiliary group with SR°_{ρ,σ} ≅ ST_ρ × ST_σ and R_{ρ,σ} ≅ S'T_ρ × SN'_σ;
- M_{ρ,σ} = M_{ρ,σ}(0)·SG, a semidirect-type product (see the previous page, (17)-(18)).

Proposition (19)-(20). The homomorphism x ↦ (Θ_G(x), Θ_ρ(x), Θ_σ(x), χ(x), ε_2(x), ε_3(x)) from M_{ρ,σ} to G × T_ρ × N'_σ × G_m × μ_2 (× μ_2) is a monomorphism. Its image is the set of systems (U, a, b, μ, ε, ε') with μ = δ_G(U) = δ_σ(b) = ε'·δ_ρ(a) and ε = ε_σ(b).

Corollary (21)-(22). Dropping the redundant coordinates, x ↦ (Θ_G, Θ_ρ, Θ_σ) is still a monomorphism. Its image is cut out by δ_G(U) = δ_σ(b) and (δ_ρ(a)/δ_σ(b))^2 = 1. The NB recovers the dropped coordinates: ε = ε_σ(b), ε' = δ_ρ(a)/δ_σ(b), μ = δ_σ(b) = δ_G(U).

Proof of injectivity. Ker(Θ_G, χ, ε_2, ε_3) = SR°_{ρ,σ} ≅ ST_ρ × ST_σ. On this kernel Θ_ρ and Θ_σ are the two projections, so the total kernel is trivial.

Proof of surjectivity onto the described subgroup (next page). Locally (fppf) choose λ with λ^2 = μ = det U. Divide (U, a, b) by the image of the section λ of G_m ⊂ R_{ρ,σ}. This reduces to a_0 ∈ S'T_ρ and b_0 ∈ SN'_σ, which lie in the image because R_{ρ,σ} ≅ S'T_ρ × SN'_σ.

Remark (next page). G cannot be replaced by PG: the kernel would then be the μ_n ⊂ SG. Overall this is a structural lemma giving an explicit equational description of the auxiliary group scheme M_{ρ,σ} as a closed subgroup of a product of classical groups.
